{"name":"Fleur de Sel Caramel","hex":"#da8704","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"fleur","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["fleur-de-lis"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/fleur"},{"term":"sel","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-2 & ISO 639-3 language code for Selkup."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/sel"},{"term":"caramel","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A smooth, chewy, sticky confection made by heating sugar and other ingredients until the sugars polymerize and become sticky.","A (sometimes hardened) piece of this confection."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a yellow-brown color."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/caramel"}],"entries":[{"title":"Fleur de Sel","blocks":[{"text":"Fleur de Sel was a restaurant in the Flatiron District of Manhattan in New York City. It had received a Michelin star."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fleur_de_Sel"},{"title":"Fleur de sel","blocks":[{"text":"Fleur de sel or flor de sal is a salt that forms as a thin, delicate crust on the surface of seawater as it evaporates. Fleur de sel has been collected since ancient times, and was traditionally used as a purgative and salve. It is now used as a finishing salt to flavor and garnish food. The origin of the name is uncertain, but is perfectly in line with both meanings of fleur: flower, and the surface of something. The salt crust forms flower-like patterns of crystals which may contribute to the name."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8a/FleurDeSel.JPG/330px-FleurDeSel.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fleur_de_sel"},{"title":"Caramel","blocks":[{"text":"Caramel is a range of food ingredients made by heating sugars to high temperatures. It is used as a flavoring in puddings and desserts, as a filling in bonbons or candy bars, as a topping for ice